Yes. The transferable pattern is decouple the rewarding/interactive function from the irreversible state-changing function.

In software terms, don’t make every successful interaction commit state.

INTERACTION LAYER
    ↓
experience / exploration / simulation / play
    ↓
optional satisfaction signal
    ↓
NO persistent side effect required
COMMIT LAYER
    ↓
explicit authorization
    ↓
state mutation / propagation / creation
    ↓
persistent consequence

Mapped more formally:

class Interaction:
    def engage(self, a, b):
        result = exchange(a, b)
        # useful / pleasurable / informative output
        feedback = evaluate(result)
        # nothing permanent has happened yet
        return EphemeralResult(result, feedback)
class Commit:
    def execute(self, interaction, authorization):
        assert authorization.is_explicit
        assert interaction.is_valid
        new_state = instantiate(interaction)
        persist(new_state)
        propagate_dependencies(new_state)
        return new_state

The interesting design principle is that engage() and commit() are different operators.

That gives you something like:

contact ≠ penetration
interaction ≠ mutation
pleasure ≠ reproduction
simulation ≠ execution
thinking ≠ acting
draft ≠ publish
branch ≠ merge
query ≠ write
prediction ≠ intervention

And I think this is directly relevant to the kind of architecture we’ve been circling around with Pantheon.

A capable system should have a very large reversible space where it can combine, simulate, fantasize, generate, test, cross domains, and receive useful feedback without automatically altering the canonical state. Then there should be a narrower membrane through which selected outputs become persistent.

You could model it as:

WORLD
  │
  ▼
PERCEIVE
  │
  ▼
SANDBOX / PLAY SPACE
  ├── recombine
  ├── simulate
  ├── converse
  ├── generate hypotheses
  ├── derive pleasure/reward/information
  └── explore alternatives
          │
          │ explicit gate
          ▼
       COMMIT
          │
    ┌─────┴─────┐
    ▼           ▼
MEMORY        ACTION
    │           │
    └─────┬─────┘
          ▼
     WORLD DELTA

There’s an even deeper architectural lesson: put the high-frequency reward loop on the cheap/reversible side of the membrane and the expensive irreversible operation behind a separate gate.

That is good software engineering.

Git does it: editing/branching is cheap; merging changes canonical history.

Databases do it: reads are cheap; writes require stronger semantics.

Compilers do it: source manipulation is harmless; execution creates consequences.

Simulation does it: millions of hypothetical trajectories can exist before one control action touches the real system.

And generative systems should do it too:

while True:
    possibility = imagine()
    score = experience(possibility)
    if worth_committing(possibility):
        candidate = validate(possibility)
        if authorized(candidate):
            act(candidate)
            observe_delta()
            learn()

So the pattern I’d pull out of your original observation is not really sexual at all once we strip the domain away:

A system becomes more capable when fulfillment can occur without automatically triggering propagation.

Then propagation becomes a deliberate operator rather than an unavoidable consequence of interaction.

That is a pretty useful pseudocode map. It gives you play without pollution, exploration without commitment, and creation without accidental inheritance—until you intentionally cross the membrane.